<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yenisei River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yenisei is one of the great rivers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>, flowing north from the mountains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> through central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to the Kara Sea of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arctic-ocean">Arctic Ocean</a>. Its upper valley, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minusinsk-basin">Minusinsk Basin</a> and Tuva, was the homeland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yenisei-kyrgyz">Yenisei Kyrgyz</a>, who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-turkic">Old Turkic</a> runic inscriptions along its banks. Russian explorers reached it in the early 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yenisei-river.jpg" alt="Yenisei River" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vyacheslav Bukharov · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yenisei_River_near_Krasnoyarsk_(2026-03-05)_-_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
